.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions manipulate ELF sections within an
.Vt elfobj_t
handle.
Sections are the fundamental organizational unit of ELF files,
containing code, data, symbol tables, relocation entries, and other
information.
.Ss Section Types
The
.Fa type
argument to
.Fn elf_add_section
specifies the section type.
Standard types include:
.Bl -tag -width "SHT_PROGBITS"
.It Dv SHT_NULL
Section header entry is unused.
.It Dv SHT_PROGBITS
Section contains program-defined contents.
.It Dv SHT_SYMTAB
Section contains a symbol table.
.It Dv SHT_STRTAB
Section contains a string table.
.It Dv SHT_RELA
Section contains relocation entries with explicit addends.
.It Dv SHT_REL
Section contains relocation entries without explicit addends.
.It Dv SHT_DYNAMIC
Section contains dynamic linking information.
.It Dv SHT_NOTE
Section contains note information.
.It Dv SHT_NOBITS
Section occupies no file space but has a virtual address.
.It Dv SHT_DYNSYM
Section contains a dynamic symbol table.
.El
.Ss Section Flags
The
.Fa flags
argument to
.Fn elf_add_section
is a bitmask of the following values:
.Bl -tag -width "SHF_EXECINSTR"
.It Dv SHF_WRITE
Section is writable during program execution.
.It Dv SHF_ALLOC
Section occupies memory during execution.
.It Dv SHF_EXECINSTR
Section contains executable instructions.
.It Dv SHF_MERGE
Section contains data that can be merged.
.It Dv SHF_STRINGS
Section contains null-terminated strings.
.It Dv SHF_GROUP
Section is a member of a section group.
.It Dv SHF_TLS
Section contains thread-local storage.
.El
.Ss Functions
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn elf_add_section "obj" "name" "type" "flags"
Creates a new section with the given
.Fa name ,
.Fa type ,
and
.Fa flags .
The section is appended to the object's section list.
Returns a pointer to the new section, or NULL on failure.
.Pp
The object must be mutable (created with
.Fn elf_create
and not yet finalized).
.It Fn elf_find_section "obj" "name"
Searches for a section by name.
Returns the section handle if found, or NULL if no section with the
given name exists.
.It Fn elf_section_set_data "section" "data" "size"
Sets the contents of a section.
The data is copied into internal storage; the caller's buffer may be
freed after this call.
.Pp
For
.Dv SHT_NOBITS
sections (such as
.Pa .bss ) ,
the
.Fa data
argument is ignored and only the
.Fa size
is recorded; no file space is allocated.
.Pp
Passing
.Fa size
as 0 clears any existing section data.
.It Fn elf_section_set_align "section" "align"
Sets section alignment in bytes.
.It Fn elf_section_set_type "section" "type"
Updates the section type.
.It Fn elf_section_set_flags "section" "flags"
Updates the section flags bitmask.
.It Fn elf_section_set_group "section" "group" "comdat"
Marks section as part of a section group and optionally COMDAT.
.It Fn elf_section_set_merge "section" "entsize" "strings"
Enables merge semantics and optional string-merge behavior.
.It Fn elf_section_set_tls "section" "enable"
Enables or disables
.Dv SHF_TLS
on the section.
.It Fn elf_section_set_note_info "section" "note_type" "note_name"
Configures note metadata and sets section type to
.Dv SHT_NOTE .
.It Fn elf_remove_section "obj" "section"
Removes a section and compacts section indices.
.It Fn elf_reorder_section "obj" "section" "new_index"
Moves a section to a new order position.
.It Fn elf_section_count "obj"
Returns the number of sections in the object.
.It Fn elf_section_name "section"
Returns the section name.
The returned string is owned by the object and must not be freed.
.It Fn elf_section_type "section"
Returns the section type
.Pq one of the SHT_* constants .
.It Fn elf_section_flags "section"
Returns the section flags
.Pq a bitmask of SHF_* constants .
.It Fn elf_section_size "section"
Returns the section size in bytes.
For
.Dv SHT_NOBITS
sections, this is the virtual size.
.It Fn elf_section_data "section" "size_out"
Returns a pointer to the section contents.
If
.Fa size_out
is non-NULL, the size is stored there.
For
.Dv SHT_NOBITS
sections, NULL is returned and the size is stored in
.Fa size_out .
.Pp
The returned pointer is owned by the object and remains valid until
the object is closed or the section data is replaced.
.El
.Ss Section Ordering
Sections are maintained in the order they are added.
When writing an ELF file, the library automatically generates the
required internal sections
.Pq Pa .symtab , Pa .strtab , Pa .shstrtab
and any relocation sections.
.Pp
The first section (index 0) is always the null section header.
User-added sections begin at index 1.
.Ss Alignment Handling
Each section has an alignment constraint stored in the
.Va addralign
field.
The library automatically aligns section offsets when writing.
.Pp
For sections created with
.Fn elf_add_section ,
the default alignment is 1.
Use
.Fn elf_section_set_align
to set a different alignment.
.Ss TLS Sections
Thread-Local Storage (TLS) sections are marked with
.Dv SHF_TLS .
These sections contain data that should have a separate instance per thread.
The runtime allocates TLS blocks and manages access via the
thread pointer or TLS register.
.Pp
TLS sections typically have the following characteristics:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Type is
.Dv SHT_PROGBITS
or
.Dv SHT_NOBITS
.Pq for .tbss .
.It
Flags include
.Dv SHF_TLS
and usually
.Dv SHF_ALLOC .
.It
Alignment is typically 8 or 16 bytes.
.El
.Ss COMDAT Groups
COMDAT groups allow multiple object files to define the same section
without causing duplicate symbol errors at link time.
Sections in a COMDAT group are marked with
.Dv SHF_GROUP .
.Pp
The current version of
.Nm libelfobj
has limited support for COMDAT groups:
.Bl -bullet
.It
COMDAT sections can be read from input files.
.It
Group signatures are not automatically processed.
.It
Group-based garbage collection is not implemented.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn elf_add_section
and
.Fn elf_find_section
return a pointer to a section handle on success, or NULL on failure.
.Pp
.Fn elf_section_set_data
returns
.Dv ELF_OK
on success, or an appropriate error code on failure.
.Pp
.Fn elf_section_count
returns the number of sections (may be 0 for an empty object).
.Pp
.Fn elf_section_name ,
.Fn elf_section_type ,
.Fn elf_section_flags ,
and
.Fn elf_section_size
return the requested attribute.
For NULL section pointers, the behavior is undefined.
.Pp
.Fn elf_section_data
returns a pointer to section data or NULL for
.Dv SHT_NOBITS
sections or empty sections.
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn elf_add_section
may fail with:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Dv ELF_ERR_OOM
Memory allocation failed.
.It Dv ELF_ERR_STATE
The object is read-only or already finalized.
.El
.Pp
.Fn elf_section_set_data
may fail with:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Dv ELF_ERR_OOM
Memory allocation failed.
.It Dv ELF_ERR_STATE
The object is read-only or already finalized.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Creating Multiple Sections
.Bd -literal
#include <elfobj.h>

int main(void) {
    elfobj_t *obj;
    elf_section_t *text, *data, *bss;
    unsigned char code[] = { 0xB8, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0xC3 };
    int values[] = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };

    obj = elf_create(ET_REL, EM_386, ELFOBJ_CLASS_32, ELFOBJ_ENDIAN_LE);
    if (!obj) return 1;

    /* Create .text section for code */
    text = elf_add_section(obj, ".text", SHT_PROGBITS,
                           SHF_ALLOC | SHF_EXECINSTR);
    if (!text) goto err;
    if (elf_section_set_data(text, code, sizeof(code)) != ELF_OK)
        goto err;

    /* Create .data section for initialized data */
    data = elf_add_section(obj, ".data", SHT_PROGBITS,
                           SHF_ALLOC | SHF_WRITE);
    if (!data) goto err;
    if (elf_section_set_data(data, values, sizeof(values)) != ELF_OK)
        goto err;

    /* Create .bss section for uninitialized data */
    bss = elf_add_section(obj, ".bss", SHT_NOBITS,
                          SHF_ALLOC | SHF_WRITE);
    if (!bss) goto err;
    /* For NOBITS, we set size but no data */
    if (elf_section_set_data(bss, NULL, 1024) != ELF_OK)
        goto err;

    if (elf_write_file(obj, "example.o") != ELF_OK) goto err;
    elf_close(obj);
    return 0;

err:
    elf_close(obj);
    return 1;
}
.Ed
.Ss Finding and Inspecting Sections
.Bd -literal
#include <elfobj.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    elfobj_t *obj;
    elf_section_t *sec;
    elf_err_t err;
    size_t size;
    const void *data;

    if (argc < 2) return 1;

    err = elf_open(argv[1], &obj);
    if (err != ELF_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Cannot open %s: %s\\n", argv[1], elf_errstr(err));
        return 1;
    }

    /* Find the .text section */
    sec = elf_find_section(obj, ".text");
    if (sec) {
        printf(".text section found:\\n");
        printf("  Type: %u\\n", elf_section_type(sec));
        printf("  Flags: 0x%llx\\n",
               (unsigned long long)elf_section_flags(sec));
        printf("  Size: %llu bytes\\n",
               (unsigned long long)elf_section_size(sec));

        data = elf_section_data(sec, &size);
        if (data && size > 0) {
            printf("  First 16 bytes:");
            for (size_t i = 0; i < 16 && i < size; i++) {
                printf(" %02x", ((const unsigned char *)data)[i]);
            }
            printf("\\n");
        }
    } else {
        printf(".text section not found\\n");
    }

    elf_close(obj);
    return 0;
}
.Ed
.Ss Iterating All Sections
.Bd -literal
#include <elfobj.h>
#include <stdio.h>

static const char *section_type_name(uint32_t type) {
    switch (type) {
    case SHT_NULL:     return "NULL";
    case SHT_PROGBITS: return "PROGBITS";
    case SHT_SYMTAB:   return "SYMTAB";
    case SHT_STRTAB:   return "STRTAB";
    case SHT_RELA:     return "RELA";
    case SHT_REL:      return "REL";
    case SHT_NOBITS:   return "NOBITS";
    case SHT_DYNSYM:   return "DYNSYM";
    default:           return "UNKNOWN";
    }
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    elfobj_t *obj;
    elf_err_t err;
    size_t i, count;

    if (argc < 2) return 1;

    err = elf_open(argv[1], &obj);
    if (err != ELF_OK) return 1;

    count = elf_section_count(obj);
    printf("Found %zu sections:\\n", count);

    /* Note: This example requires storing section pointers during
     * iteration. In practice, use elf_find_section for known names
     * or maintain your own index. */
    for (i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        /* Sections must be accessed by name or stored during creation.
         * See elfobj.3 for object model details. */
    }

    elf_close(obj);
    return 0;
}
